  "items": [
    {
      "external_id": "C11-CHEM-CH9-9.3",
      "chapter": "pk-punjab/xi/chemistry/c11-chem/ch9",
      "topic": "pk-punjab/xi/chemistry/c11-chem/ch9/9.3",
      "title": "9.3 Ionic product of water",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 192,
          "pdf_to": 193,
          "note": "Topic 9.3 with Table 9.2, printed pages 192-193."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Pure water is a poor conductor of electricity but its conductance is measurable. Water undergoes self-ionization reversibly as follows,"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{H2O_{(\\ell)} + H2O_{(\\ell)} <=> H3O^+_{(aq)} + OH^-_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "H₂O(ℓ) + H₂O(ℓ) ⇌ H₃O⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Net reaction}\\qquad \\ce{H2O_{(\\ell)} <=> H^+_{(aq)} + OH^-_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "Net reaction: H₂O(ℓ) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The equilibrium constant for this reaction can be written as follows:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "K_c = \\frac{[\\ce{H+}]\\,[\\ce{OH-}]}{[\\ce{H2O}]} = 1.8 \\times 10^{-16}\\ \\text{mol dm}^{-3}",
            "text_alt": "Kc = [H⁺][OH⁻] / [H₂O] = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁶ mol dm⁻³"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The concentration of H₂O, i.e., [H₂O] in pure water may be calculated to be 1000 g dm⁻³ divided by 18 g mol⁻¹ giving 55.5 mol dm⁻³. Since, water is present in very large excess and very few of its molecules undergo ionization, so its concentration remains effectively constant. Constant concentration of water is taken on L.H.S. and multiplied with Kc to get another constant called Kw."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\begin{aligned} K_c[\\ce{H2O}] &= [\\ce{H+}]\\,[\\ce{OH-}] \\\\ \\text{or}\\quad 1.8 \\times 10^{-16} \\times 55.5 = 1.01 \\times 10^{-14} &= [\\ce{H+}]\\,[\\ce{OH-}] \\end{aligned}",
            "text_alt": "Kc[H₂O] = [H⁺][OH⁻]; or 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁶ × 55.5 = 1.01 × 10⁻¹⁴ = [H⁺][OH⁻]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "This 1.01 x 10⁻¹⁴ is called Kw of water at 25°C."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\begin{aligned} K_c[\\ce{H2O}] &= [\\ce{H+}]\\,[\\ce{OH-}] \\\\ \\text{So,}\\quad K_w[\\ce{H2O}] &= [\\ce{H+}]\\,[\\ce{OH-}] = 10^{-14}\\ \\text{at}\\ 25^\\circ\\text{C} \\end{aligned}",
            "text_alt": "Kc [H₂O] = [H⁺][OH⁻]; So, Kw [H₂O] = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25°C",
            "book_errors": [
              "Kw [H₂O]",
              "K_w[\\ce{H2O}]"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "The print gives Kw [H₂O] = [H⁺][OH⁻]. Kw already contains [H₂O]: Kw = Kc[H₂O] = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25°C."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "definition",
            "title": "Ionic product of water",
            "text": "Kw is called ionic product of water or dissociation constant of water."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The value of Kw increases almost 75 times when temperature is increased from 0°C to 100°C."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For neutral water"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\begin{aligned} [\\ce{H+}] &= [\\ce{OH-}] \\\\ \\text{or}\\quad [\\ce{H+}][\\ce{H+}] &= 10^{-14} \\\\ [\\ce{H+}]^2 &= 10^{-14}\\quad (\\text{at } 25^\\circ\\text{C}) \\\\ [\\ce{H+}] &= 10^{-7}\\ \\text{mol dm}^{-3} \\\\ \\text{and}\\quad [\\ce{OH-}] &= 10^{-7}\\ \\text{mol dm}^{-3} \\end{aligned}",
            "text_alt": "[H⁺] = [OH⁻]; or [H⁺][H⁺] = 10⁻¹⁴; [H⁺]² = 10⁻¹⁴ (at 25°C); [H⁺] = 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³ and [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The effect of temperature on Kw is shown in Table 9.2. Whenever some quantity of acid or base is added to water, then Kw remains the same, but [H⁺] and [OH⁻] are no more equal."
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "caption": "Table 9.2 Kw at various temperature.",
            "header": [
              "Temp. (°C)",
              "Kw"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "0",
                "0.11 × 10¹⁴"
              ],
              [
                "10",
                "0.30 × 10¹⁴"
              ],
              [
                "25",
                "1.0 × 10¹⁴"
              ],
              [
                "40",
                "3.00 × 10¹⁴"
              ],
              [
                "100",
                "7.5 × 10¹⁴"
              ]
            ],
            "book_errors": [
              "0.11 × 10¹⁴",
              "0.30 × 10¹⁴",
              "1.0 × 10¹⁴",
              "3.00 × 10¹⁴",
              "7.5 × 10¹⁴"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "Table 9.2 prints the powers as 10¹⁴. Kw is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 25°C, as the text says, so the values are 0.11, 0.30, 1.0, 3.00 and 7.5 × 10⁻¹⁴."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In case of addition of small amount of acid."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "[\\ce{H+}] > [\\ce{OH-}]",
            "text_alt": "[H⁺] > [OH⁻]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "While in the case of addition of few drops of a base."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "[\\ce{OH-}] > [\\ce{H+}]",
            "text_alt": "[OH⁻] > [H⁺]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "During both of these additions, the values of Kw will remain the same, i.e. 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
